Z||000007||treyb79||04-29-2001||04:25 AM||||I have an '88 Formula as well (same color too, we have twins) ... 2.8 V6 w/ 5 speed Getrag ... Aside from stalling out, I have very similar symptoms with my Fiero.  Odo. is just over 110,000 carefully driven miles.  I acquired this vechile recently from a very good friend, and he told me that the reason for the richness at idle was due to an aftermarket chip he had installed.  Could this be true?  I also have the same black junk that shoots out of my pipes too when you rev it at idle.  <P>As for coolant sensors, I'm not sure as to their status but I will ask him and see.  If it is the fuel injector switch, how is that checked/replaced?  (Sorry, I should invest in a factory service manual, but this is a very recent thing, and I have yet to sink $100 on one :-) )<P>Thanks for any help,<P>Trey<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||86 GT||04-29-2001||07:04 AM||||My driveway is spotted with soot marks from start ups. We have a new cat and exhaust, no codes showing and recently passed Ontario's clean air emissions testing. Car runs great. Mileage is 300 kilo or 180 miles per tank, compression is 95 plus or minus each cylinder. Soot does not smell of fuel, it is more like condensation. My tailpipes are covered in back soot. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||87GTZ34||04-29-2001||12:51 PM||||The cold start valve is supplied with power ONLY during crank from the starter solenoid.  The cold start injector should not continue to provide fuel once the ignition switch is released. <BR>The following procedure can be used to diagnose the system.  You may want to perform all of the cold tests first then the hot ones so you don't have to let the engine cool to ambient temp in-between tests.<P>A simple test is to: warm up the engine; shut it down, disconnect the the harness on the cold-start injector itself and start/run the engine again.  If performance/smoothness is notable then control of the injector is faulty (leaving it on).  <BR>If there is no notable difference then try the following test.<BR>If the injector is leaking then you will need to: (with a cold engine) disconnect the main injectors at the valve cover harness connectors; put the switch to the on position and note fuel pressure (oh did I mention you need a FI fuel pressure gauge hooked up).  The pressure should remain steady once up to pressure.  Next, crank the engine (it will try to start but not run), and the fuel pressure should drop (about 3psi) once the starter starts cranking.<BR>Reconnect the main injector harness start the engine and warm it up to temp then repeat the above test.  Where you got a pressure drop before while cranking, it should now remain steady.<P>If you suspect the main injectors are leaking then b4 you unplug the main harness, put the ignition on, and note the fuel pressure.  Again it should not drop.<P>Good luck<P>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 04-29-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 04-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ship Rider||04-29-2001||02:50 PM||||Mine had similar symptoms that were fixed with the injectors being rebuilt.  Only $24.5 per,||||reg||1
